 the situation  and how quickly the situation has  evolved with the omicron variant  i'll let dr moore and matt speak to some  of the details in a moment but i want to  assure ontarians that we're starting to  see glimmers of hope  the sacrifices you are making now have  meant we are beginning to see signs of  stabilization  omicron cases are expected to peak this  month  with the peak in hospitalizations and  icu admissions to follow  new hospitalizations are slowing and are  now doubling closer to every two weeks  experience in our hospitals has  confirmed omicron is not as severe as  delta  with far fewer patients requiring  intensive care than in previous waves  despite much higher rates of  transmission in the community  over the coming days and weeks we expect  these trends to continue  giving us more confidence as we plan for  what comes next  but i do want to be clear  february will continue to pose  challenges especially for our hospitals  as people continue to require care for  covet 19.  but our goal has always been to ensure  capacity is there to provide care for  those who need it  given current trends we are increasingly  confident in our ability to do so  ontarians have also continued to make  good use of this time by rolling up  their sleeves  and we are leading the country in  getting boosters into arms  our government issued a call to action  to get as many vaccines into arms as  possible and team ontario has answered  that call  the capacity is in place and  appointments are available through four  ontarians who want to get their vaccines  sooner  i continue to urge everyone to get their  first  second and booster shots as soon as they  can  another exciting positive step is the  recent health canada approval of  paxlovid an antiviral medication  we are expecting to receive very limited  supply of just over ten thousand courses  of treatment for january  we are working with ontario health to  distribute this supply at a number of  sites across the province as soon as  it's received helping to further protect  our hospital capacity by prioritizing  those at highest risk of severe outcomes  so i know that this has been a very long  and difficult road for the people of  ontario  you have shown remarkable resolve in the  face of considerable  adversity each time you've risen to the  challenge  and i have no doubt that you will do the  same now  we will  we will get through this together  ontarians remain committed as we always  have by taking a cautious and gradual  approach  guided by the evidence  today we want to provide some of the  data that we are using to help inform  the cautious approach that will guide us  over the weeks ahead  before i hand things over to dr moore i  want to respond to some extremely  concerning reports that some doctors are  spreading misinformation about vaccines  at a time when it's never been more  important for ontarians to have  confidence in the safety and  effectiveness of vaccines  this is unacceptable  i will be sending a letter to the  college of physicians and surgeons of  ontario urging them to do everything  that is possible to put an end to this  behavior  they should consider all options in  doing so including reviewing the  licenses of physicians found to be  spreading misinformation  this behavior takes away from the  unceasing efforts of the vast majority  of physicians who are working to protect  us all  i want all ontarians to know that our  vaccines are safe  and effective  they're the best protection you have  against covid and omicron  please please get vaccinated as soon as  you're able  thank you very much and i will now turn  things over to dr moore  thank you minister elliot and good  morning everyone bonjourne tus  as the minister just indicated the  sacrifices ontarians have been making  are having an impact  i'm starting to have more hope and  cautious optimism  for the first time in several weeks the  number of hospitalizations and cases in  the intensive care unit is increasing at  a slower pace the severity of omicron  coupled with the protection offered by  vaccines has meant that the length of  stay for hospitalizations is now five  days compared to nine days for the delta  variant  we are also now starting to see our pcr  test positivity stabilize between 20 and  25  when considered alongside other data we  we track such as capacity and length of  stay in hospitals in icu this is an  encouraging sign  that we may  may be reaching the peak of omicron  transmission in ontario in the coming  weeks  while this gives me more hope we must  remain cautious and humble in the face  of this aggressive virus  and the most important thing we can all  do as the minister has said is get  vaccinated  prevention is the key with additional  public health measures currently in  place our our public health and  healthcare partners have taken advantage  of this time to get even more shots into  arms in just over two weeks thank you to  all of our vaccinators over  and fifty thousand ontarians have  received a first dose  and one million doses have been  administered in total  more than 725  000 people have gotten their booster  dose and ontario is leading the country  in third doses administered  5.7 million ontarians have stepped up  come forward and received their third  doses  77  of our over 70s  are now protected  better with their third doses so thank  you to all those that have come forward  and thank all the vaccinators across  ontario  vaccines remain the best tool to protect  ourselves against severe outcomes of  covet 19  including hospitalization and admission  to intensive care  currently 50 percent  of the individuals in the intensive care  units across ontario are unvaccinated  and yet the unvaccinated only make up 10  of our population  this alone demonstrates the positive  protection that vaccination can provide  against these severe outcomes and the  benefits of vaccination against severe  outcomes have been shown across all age  groups that is why i continue to  strongly encourage the parents of our  youngest eligible ontarians those 5 to  11 years of age to come forward and get  their children vaccinated  currently  and thankfully 50.4 percent of children  have received their first dose but we  want to see that number continue to  climb  and for parents to continue booking and  coming for their second doses the  pediatric pfizer vaccine for children is  safe  it's effective and it provides strong  protection against covet 19 and its  variants  boosters are incredibly important and as  a reminder it's very important for those  who are older in our community who have  any any underlying medical disease for  our health care workers and pregnant  individuals and those that are  immunocompromised  it cannot be emphasized enough just how  critical vaccination is to reducing our  personal and community risk and  protecting our health care system  so thank you ontarians for embracing  vaccination and for making the  sacrifices that you have over the last  several weeks  together we've made an impact and we've  made a difference  and together these efforts will chart  our path forward towards a safe and  gradual  reopening we must stay cautious we must  stay disciplined and never underestimate  this virus as we look to take the small  steps forward thank you and i'll now  pass it over to matt anderson  all right good morning  thank you minister elliot uh thank you  dr moore i'm going to take a few minutes  just to provide a brief update on our  health system and our capacity and some  of the challenges of this wave  overall as you've already heard from the  minister and dr moore we are seeing some  encouraging signs  but we remain in a very challenging  environment and we're not out of the  woods yet  just to highlight a few of the measures  that we're monitoring um that do point  uh to some hopeful signs  as you've heard from dr moore we're  seeing relatively fewer people requiring  intensive care  over the last few weeks the total icu  occupancy has remained relatively  constant  today we have a total of 571 covid  positive patients in our icus compared  to a high of just under 900 at the peak  of wave 3.  we're also seeing that patients have a  shorter length of stay  in our icu  and this is helping to limit the overall  rise of  the numbers in our icus  on the acute care side where we have our  our biggest challenge is in our ward  beds or hospital beds we currently have  more than 4 100 coveted positive  patients in our hospitals  this is the highest total of  hospitalizations we've seen throughout  the pandemic and over the last seven  days  fortunately we are starting to see some  slowing of that growth  as noted by dr moore it's still growing  but the rate that is growing each day  seems to be slowing  these are encouraging signs  however it's important to remember that  even while with these key measures are  trending in the right direction  we don't feel that in the hospitals we  don't realize these benefits for a  number of more weeks  we still have hospitals that are under  very challenging circumstances as we  deal with these rising numbers  as the minister has indicated our health  system  will likely continue to experience  challenges through february  a particular unique challenge of this  wave has been related to  sick call  as it relates to kovid in many of our  healthcare settings  it's affecting all sectors and continues  to add strain on the remaining staff  this includes our community care centers  our community support service  home care  and hospitals across the province  primary care has also been affected  however they continue to do amazing work  to support our vaccination efforts  and we are seeing that they are  continuing to try to grow their  in-person  supportive care  thankfully we are seeing some signs  that absentee due to covid may be  stabilizing  we do have a number of agencies across  the province saying that they're  experiencing that they now have more  staff coming back  then are on sick call that day  an encouraging sign but again something  we're going to have to watch  we continue to face pressures in some of  our hot spot hospitals  we do have a number of hospitals that  are operating at over 100 percent  occupancy in their ward beds  through our regional incident management  systems we have been transferring  patients to try to load balance and to  try to ensure a safe duty of care for  all patients who are coming into all  people who are coming into our hospitals  last year last week alone  we did over 100 transfers  in other ways  as in other ways we are using the full  resources of our provincial health  system this has been key to our efforts  to ensure ontarians get the care they  need  we recognize that with the difficult  decisions that we've had to make over  the last few weeks this has had a  tremendous impact on all of us and on  our families most of us if not all of us  know someone who is waiting for care  these are difficult choices through this  phase of the pandemic  along with our ministry and government  colleagues we remain focused on  addressing delayed care as soon as we  possibly can  in closing i just want to echo the  minister's thanks to our health care  workers  our system has been under strain for  almost two years now and yet our front  line has not wavered in their dedication  and commitment to patients  thank you to our health care providers  across the entire health care system  our community care primary care  long-term care home care and acute care  hospital workers  all of you have continued to do your  part to keep our system running and  caring for patients thank you again for  the opportunity to address you  operator over to the first question  please  your first question comes from allison  jones with the canadian press please go  ahead  oh hi thanks very much uh my first  question is probably best put to  minister elliot  um we're here hearing the message today  obviously that there's some very early  positive signs some glimmers of hope  as you say  you say that there's a positive  announcement coming later this week on  restrictions is that perhaps a little  premature to talk about when schools are  reopening this week and that could  potentially  add another wrinkle to this  could it possibly drive up transmission  in a way that erases some of these gains  allison i'm going to refer that question  to to dr moore to speak about  what  challenges we're facing with the opening  of schools and  nothing specific about what next steps  will take but just to speak about the  issues you've just raised  hi allison thank you for that question  well i am excited to have schools open  and i hope all parents  understand that we're opening them with  as many layers of protection for the  students  as possible including daily screening  the rat test should be able to be  distributed now that the schools are  open after the snow storms  and the mass that we provided so we have  as many layers of protection as we can  in the school setting to reduce the risk  of transmission uh we'll clearly be  monitoring that situation  i i am looking forward to the government  announcement i am confident that it will  be phased it will be gradual it will be  based on data  and will follow the prudence that  they've done in the past  on that staged and phased reopening we  will continue though to monitor any  risks of increased transmission in the  community  as a measure schools certainly haven't  had a significant impact on transmission  in the past and i do believe the  measures we put in place should minimize  them  as best we can as a society and we've  together made schools as safe as we can  and again happy to have students back in  the classroom for their mental physical  social and educational well-being  follow-up yeah thanks this is also for  for you dr moore um i had asked you last  week about kovid deaths in the province  and unfortunately we've seen  another very high number  of deaths reported today and i'm  wondering if you're able to share any  more information today on  what's leading to this whether  it's still the tail end of delta  infections or if omicron is driving this  is this  mostly  you know is it based on vaccination  status is it based on age group is there  anything more you can give us to help us  understand what's happening well i i  applaud your question because i had the  same questions myself and we are  continuing to work with all of our  partners to get as best data to  ontarians as possible so we're reviewing  the death certification process we're  reviewing whether the death is causative  or associated with covet 19 we're trying  to clarify if individuals have been  vaccinated or not  death and i hate  talking statistically about death i know  each individual is a member of someone's  family and and i'm absolutely regret um  the deaths but  statistically i do believe  that a significant proportion of the  deaths are related to delta as we all  remember we had that delta rise at the  end of december  and then omicron took over delta is a  much more severe pathogen had a higher  death rate associated with it  and  we're seeking to clarify  and differentiate omicron versus delta  so bear with us  these are often  difficult  elements to tease out  across a health system to be able to  give the best and most reliable data to  ontarians and decision makers but if you  you ask me i do believe that the vast  majority are related to delta because  as you know the someone gets admitted to  hospital then often they'll get  transferred to the intensive care unit  then  they if they deteriorate they get  intubated  to be able to maintain  oxygenation levels uh and and then um uh  they either pull through or sadly a  significant proportion die uh and that  process often takes four to six weeks uh  and we're just trying to get you uh and  all ontarians better data in in that  regard  next question  your next question comes from  richard southern with city news 680.  hi minister uh the premier has said and  i think dr moore kind of said the same  thing that we're going back to other  levels of restrictions it sounds to me  like there's already a plan in place can  you number one give us any idea what  that plan is today but number two  what data is this going to be based on  we haven't seen any data from schools  because they just went back in session  today how can you make a decision  minister about lifting restrictions when  we have seen no data from schools  well we will have more to say  about  any movement with respect to  restrictions later on this week  but  i would say with respect to schools we  are very pleased that the students are  going back for all the reasons indicated  by dr moore and that there's very little  evidence that there's  high transmission just in schools  themselves we know that there's high  levels of community transmission right  now but in schools themselves  as students go there they have been  made as safe as possible with the the  heppy filters with the hepa filters with  the  number of rapid tests that are going  into the schools with the n95 mask being  available for teachers and child care  workers we are taking every step  possible to make sure that when our  students go safe because safely back to  school that they will remain healthy  follow-up  you mentioned maths minister we've heard  from a number of parents who say the  masks provided to students don't fit  correctly i'm wondering if you can speak  to how important a a properly fitting  mask is number one number two you say  icu uh uh occupancy has remained  constant i'm wondering if dr moore could  speak to if that has anything to do with  the high death rate 60 more ontarians  losing their their lives to coven today  okay well thank you i'll refer that  questions to dr moore then  so the first question in regard to mass  quality um the mass that we've provided  the three ply masks uh to to students  across ontario um are to provide an  alternate for students if they uh  want to use  those masks uh and um you're absolutely  right a key component is that they fit  well so one would hope uh in the  educational sector uh they're helping  students uh ensure that the mass are  appropriately fitting uh and uh all  masks have to cover the nose right down  to the chin uh we should wash our hands  before wash our hands after uh and uh  it is the best fitting mask  that all children should be wearing so  if that's a quality assurance issue that  we have to follow up with absolutely we  will  in partnership with the ministry of  education in terms of the icu one of the  most important markers that we've been  following and sadly you're absolutely  right a certain proportion do succumb to  covid19 has been the length of stay  and the length of stay  had a peak  many  months ago  in the 20s  20 days which gave us an understanding  of the complexity of care that some  individuals required in multi-organ  failure that some individuals  had with kidney failure and or bone  marrow suppression  and or liver injury  so  now though we're seeing a much different  picture the length of stay is uh coming  down it's stabilizing at six and seven  days which tells me we're providing the  right care to the right ontarians at the  right time  and i do think with omicron we'll see  less lethality that we're seeing that  trend as we compare to previous waves  because  the virulence is less but some  individuals still need  intensive care  services and mechanical ventilation  especially if they've got any underlying  illnesses and hence our mantra of please  if you've got any underlying illness no  matter what age 18 and above please come  forward and get your booster dose it's  very important as a marker of prevention  against severe outcomes  so i hope that answered those two  different questions  next question  your next question comes from erica  villa with global news please go ahead  thanks so much for taking my question uh  this question is for dr moore you've  previously said that you wanted to see a  plateau uh in terms of numbers is there  a certain decrease or a number that  we're looking to to see within  hospitalizations  before really initiating that plan here  in ontario and are you also planning on  ramping up testing for that as well so  it is a series of data points that we're  watching so  a key metric is the total number of  patients with causative  admissions to the intensive care unit  and  require and or requiring mechanical  ventilation and their length of stay  and the total number of patients that  are causative in hospital from covet 19  and their length of stay so that the  ability of the health care system in  ontario to care for those that have the  most severe illness those are key and  important metrics to also we still have  55 000 ontarians that are getting pcr  tested every day  and thanks to all of those that are  coming forward to our assessment centers  we're looking at the percentage of those  tests that are positive it's it went up  to 30  percent 31  it's come now to the 20 to 25 range so  that's a marker of the percentage of  tests that are positive that's a again  another key metric the other key metrics  we're watching certainly  is the health care capacity uh in  ontario so we knew many workers would be  absent uh due to themselves getting  coveted 19 principally from the  community  and we needed to ensure that all  ontarians would receive care at the  right time at the right place by the  right staff  and as matt has mentioned to us we're  starting to see a health care  provider  stabilize and we are able to provide  care despite 100 occupancy in some of  our settings  and the number of outbreaks in  particular in our long-term care sector  is is another key metric  because  patients have to move across our system  from the acute care sector to community  hospitals and then often back to  long-term care sectors  and the more facilities that are in  outbreak the more difficult it is to  provide the right care at the right time  so i hope you're realizing it's not one  number it will be a judgment based on  all of these different metrics by  government of when to safely and  cautiously reopen  and i do believe we've done so well in  the past  and sometimes we've had to stabilize at  one phase of the reopening and go longer  sometimes we've gone a little shorter  but we've always followed the data and i  hope that i've given you a brief  synopsis of some of the key metrics that  we're following  follow-up thank you for that my next  question is for the minister for my  colleague karen lieberman uh minister  ontario has uh pledged to provide n95  masks for education staff and rapid  tests for uh to staff and students will  this be enough coupled with other  measures like the hepa filters to keep  schools open during this latest wave  well we i i will say that we've taken  every step that is possible to protect  our students and staff and and daycare  workers as well having the hepa filters  is really important we're putting  seventy seven hundred thousand we're  adding another several thousand we are  uh distributing the uh rapid antigen  tests to teachers and students so that  they can  monitor these tests will be supplemented  as time goes by  we're  we've increased the number of cleaning  and disinfecting processes that we're  taking so we're doing everything that we  can to protect our students because it  is really important for their mental and  physical health as well as their  educational needs for them to be back in  school  next question  your next question comes from john  mcgrath with tvo please go ahead  good morning minister and uh  dr moore you've both repeatedly said  that you're doing  everything you can to protect students  as they return to school but i mean  that's not true right the the uh  co-vaccinations are not mandatory for  students the way they are for  other  vaccines uh the immunization of school  pupils act has not been  amended and even if the government  doesn't want to make that a general rule  across the province  local mohs who have asked for that power  can't do anything until the government  amends that regulation they  they don't have the power to do that so  i'm wondering if the minister could  explain why the government continues to  tie  local medical officers at hand and deny  them this  important public health tool  well many parents are taking advantage  of the fact that their children age 5 to  11 can be vaccinated  we have over  a 50 percent a number of children that  have been vaccinated with their first  dose already and more are coming forward  but with respect to the particular  regulation i will  refer that to dr moore please  i'll i'll just remind once again that  the immunization of student pupils act  is not mandatory immunization it's  mandatory reporting of your immunization  status and in the ispa  you you must provide us with  in public health for a set number of  vaccines whether your child is immunized  if you haven't had them immunized will  provide an educational module or you can  provide a medical  exemption  or  some other type of objection based on  religion or philosophy or beliefs  so we don't have mandatory immunization  policies in ontario um we are working  with their health units to ensure that  they can get line listings  electronically of  the classroom so if there is activity  within a school  they'll know the immunization status and  they'll be able to provide advice to the  school boards  and to ensure that they have the data at  their fingertips  certainly over time we will be reviewing  as we do for all measures within the  covid  our policies our protocols and our  procedures  but i do believe  health units have tools at their  disposal to be able to  consult on a regular basis and support  the opening of schools  and have data as they need it  follow-up  in terms of the testing both the the  positivity has come down a little bit  but the volume of tests conducted and  the backlog  are both down substantially from where  they were several weeks ago  pardon me if i  missed something specific on this but is  the government intending to  expand eligibility for pcr testing again  so thank you for that question so as of  today the report we've been processing  53 000 tests a day so thank you to all  ontarians that have gone to assessment  centers and guided testing our turn  around time we're still not a perfectly  happy with the turnaround time of 64  percent after two days  that  sometimes will slow down the ability of  public health to respond to an outbreak  or to a scenario to be able to minimize  harm so we're absolutely working on  improving the turnaround time with that  53 000  tests a day  we had a high of 58 000 on january 7th  so the system is working  in terms of being able to test those  that meet the eligibility criteria and  as we've said before we all have to  remain flexible and adaptive  if we see an increased demand for  certain populations to have access to  pcr timing we have to make sure it's  timely  that it will enable rapid decision  making by public health for treatments  and or for isolation or quarantine and  high-risk congregate settings especially  our long-term care facilities our  retirement homes  our shelters  and in our hospital sector so those were  our priorities we have a framework by  which we will approach opening uh  eligibility for further pcr testing  based on need based on access for  treatment uh and based on high-risk  populations so  we're working diligently and thank you  to all laboratories  they are having the same uh  sickness calls that other agencies are  happening i know they're working  diligently to try to process these tests  as quickly and as efficiently as  possible and thank them for all their  work i know our turnaround time will  improve uh in the coming days uh  and it was a difficult beginning of  january for most organizations and  businesses across ontario and this  clearly reflects it so to to summarize  and to finally answer your question  we're absolutely reviewing all criteria  on a regular basis of access to pcr uh  and ensuring that people get the right  test at the right time i'm i'm happy  that we've given out 3.9 million rat  tests  to parents  of children  and that they can  no longer have to do the drive down to  an assessment center  have to wait for a pcr result but can do  the test in the quiet of their home with  minimal  travel time required and have the answer  within minutes so  thank uh parents for their patience as  we distribute the rits and we will  certainly as the minister said  supplement those rats with additional  tests  on a regular basis to ensure all parents  and children have access to keep our  schools safe  last question  your final question comes from rob  ferguson with the toronto star please go  ahead  hi rob  hello everyone i'll direct this to dr  moore um last thursday when you were  asked about reopening you said you  couldn't guarantee that  january 26 would be a good day to get  rolling on it has your opinion on that  state changed  or is  is there still some caution there about  january 26th  hi robert i i do believe this is a  government decision uh the premier i i  believe um will be um  making a statement on this um we will be  continuing reviewing data to inform that  decision and i can't make any  foreshadowing of  of that statement  follow-up and this is the last question  okay we all know what the government  decision but we also know also all know  that you're the one who makes the  recommendation so i asked you  what is your opinion of that date and  especially in light of the fact that  schools are getting most schools are  getting  back into in-class learning a little bit  later than expected  well i i do think it's two weeks uh that  date is roughly two weeks after the  start of school even though with the  snowstorm we'll have to be a little more  cautious and monitoring  we're monitoring all pediatric  admissions to hospital all pediatric  admissions the intensive care unit we're  tracking that very closely as well as  the pcr test positivity in children to  have a key metric of any increased  transmission or risk within the school  settings and we'll be informing  government of any trends in the coming  days to enable them to make an informed  decision on the reopening  of ontario
 

